 ## Subtopics
 
+### [Domain-based BVH with Range Bounding Boxes](2808-domain-based-bvh-with-range-bounding-boxes.md)
+The Domain-based BVH with Range Bounding Boxes is an acceleration structure for fiber surface extraction in tetrahedral meshes, utilizing a Bounding Volume Hierarchy (BVH) over the domain to store interval ranges (RBBs) at each node. It solves the problem of efficiently identifying tetrahedra that intersect a given FSCP (Fiber Surface Control Polygon) edge by filtering candidates via 2-D intersection tests in range space. Unlike its sibling [Domain-based Octree with Range Bounding Boxes](2806-domain-based-octree-with-range-bounding-boxes.md), the BVH is not strictly domain-based, allowing it to better capture the geometry of the input polygon in range-space, resulting in fewer returned tetrahedra, smaller memory requirements, and faster queries.
+
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** Construction is $O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of tetrahedra, assuming constant work per tetrahedron to update RBBs. Query time depends on the selectivity of the RBBs; in the worst case, it visits all nodes, but typically prunes significantly [S1].
-- **Memory Complexity:** Stores one RBB per octree node. The number of nodes depends on the data distribution and parameters $n_T$ and $\alpha$.
+- **Time Complexity:** Construction is $O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of tetrahedra, assuming balanced subdivision, but can be worse due to unbalanced trees [S1]. Query time is proportional to the number of visited nodes, which depends on the selectivity of the RBB tests.
+- **Memory Complexity:** Depends on the depth and branching factor. The source notes that the octree uses more memory than the BVH variant [S1].
 - **Measured Performance:** On the EthaneDiol dataset, an octree with $\alpha=0.001$ used 649.736 MB memory, took 2.362s pre-processing, queried 35.260% of tetrahedra, and took 0.311s for extraction [S1]. Average speedup over non-accelerated algorithm: 18x [S1].
-- **Strengths:** Simple to implement; provides good culling for sparse fiber surfaces; conservative nature ensures no false negatives [S1].
-- **Limitations:** Conservative AABB test leads to false positives; unbalanced tree can lead to deep recursion; less efficient than BVH for complex FSCP shapes [S1].
+- **Strengths:** Simple hierarchical structure; effective culling of large empty regions; conservative test allows fast rejection.
+- **Limitations:** Unbalanced trees can lead to deep recursion; conservative test returns false positives; less efficient than BVH in terms of memory and query speed for complex polygons [S1].
+- **When to use:** When a simple, domain-based spatial hierarchy is preferred over a range-based BVH, or when the data distribution is regular.
+- **When not to use:** When memory is constrained or when the FSCP geometry is highly complex and requires precise culling (BVH is better) [S1].
 
 ## Background
-The method was introduced in the context of fast and exact fiber surface extraction for tetrahedral meshes [S1]. It extends the concept of using octrees to store interval ranges (RBBs) at each node for isosurface extraction [36] to the more complex case of polygonal FSCP edges, requiring 2-D intersection tests in range space [S1].
